WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.375 --> 00:00:03.169
- Okay, so let's just,

00:00:03.169 --> 00:00:05.570
let's just put it on
the table, here, okay,

00:00:05.570 --> 00:00:07.369
E.T. is frequently cited
as one of the worst

00:00:07.369 --> 00:00:09.571
video games in history.

00:00:09.571 --> 00:00:12.112
I got to read a magazine that said that my game

00:00:12.112 --> 00:00:15.290
was single-handedly
responsible for the crash

00:00:15.290 --> 00:00:18.746
of the video game
industry in the early 80s.

00:00:18.746 --> 00:00:20.373
(laughing)

00:00:20.373 --> 00:00:21.450
That's a good one.

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:23.247
People are reticent to ask me about it,

00:00:23.247 --> 00:00:24.591
they think I'm very
sensitive about it, you know,

00:00:24.591 --> 00:00:26.500
"Oh my God, you did the worst
game of all time, you know,

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:28.074
don't you wanna hide?"

00:00:28.074 --> 00:00:29.564
and it's like, no.

00:00:29.564 --> 00:00:31.224
- [Narrator] Howard Scott Warshaw.

00:00:31.224 --> 00:00:34.776
He was a video game programmer
for Atari in the early 80s.

00:00:34.776 --> 00:00:39.439
He made some of the most beloved
games for the Atari 2600.

00:00:39.439 --> 00:00:41.875
- [Howard] I made Yars'
Revenge, then I followed that

00:00:41.875 --> 00:00:44.428
with Raiders of the Lost
Ark, I had had two very

00:00:44.428 --> 00:00:47.372
successful games, both million sellers.

00:00:47.372 --> 00:00:50.163
I truly believed everything
I touched, hopefully,

00:00:50.163 --> 00:00:51.585
was gonna turn to gold
because I was gonna put

00:00:51.585 --> 00:00:54.243
everything I had into it.

00:00:54.243 --> 00:00:56.140
- [Narrator] One fateful
day, Howard received a call

00:00:56.140 --> 00:00:58.717
from the CEO of Atari, asking
him if he wanted to take on

00:00:58.717 --> 00:01:02.390
their highest profile game ever,
E.T. The Extra-Terrestrial.

00:01:02.390 --> 00:01:05.423
- [Howard] And I said, "We
definitely can do that."

00:01:05.423 --> 00:01:07.341
- [Narrator] But, there
was one major caveat.

00:01:07.341 --> 00:01:09.861
- [Howard] It had to be something
that related to the movie,

00:01:09.861 --> 00:01:11.921
and it had to be doable in five weeks.

00:01:11.921 --> 00:01:14.590
I mean, I had five weeks to do the game,

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:17.542
so I was working all the time.

00:01:17.542 --> 00:01:21.709
(digital beeping)
(upbeat music)

00:01:24.647 --> 00:01:26.146
E.T. was gonna be a basic puzzle game,

00:01:26.146 --> 00:01:28.486
with some challenges that
you run around to solve,

00:01:28.486 --> 00:01:31.827
and if I redistribute the
pieces among enough different,

00:01:31.827 --> 00:01:36.061
random places, it makes a fresh
challenge each time around,

00:01:36.061 --> 00:01:36.894
and bingo.

00:01:37.926 --> 00:01:39.375
- [Narrator] When it
was released, E.T. was

00:01:39.375 --> 00:01:42.394
an immediate success, selling
over a million copies.

00:01:42.394 --> 00:01:45.148
But a few months later,
things started to turn.

00:01:45.148 --> 00:01:47.486
- [Howard] Retailers are
starting to find that the game

00:01:47.486 --> 00:01:49.493
is not moving, it's coming
back, a lot of people

00:01:49.493 --> 00:01:50.883
are disappointed with it.

00:01:50.883 --> 00:01:52.286
- [Narrator] Because of the poor planning

00:01:52.286 --> 00:01:54.225
and tight production schedule,
Howard failed to catch

00:01:54.225 --> 00:01:56.090
a fundamental flaw in the game's design.

00:01:56.090 --> 00:01:58.786
- [Howard] There are too many
times where you make a move,

00:01:58.786 --> 00:02:01.103
you do something, and suddenly
you wind up somewhere else,

00:02:01.103 --> 00:02:02.714
now suddenly you're somewhere else,

00:02:02.714 --> 00:02:04.970
and there's too many places
in E.T. where the user

00:02:04.970 --> 00:02:09.597
is disoriented, and that's
a sin of which I am guilty,

00:02:09.597 --> 00:02:12.787
and I have been serving penance.

00:02:12.787 --> 00:02:14.065
- [Narrator] Shortly after E.T.'s release,

00:02:14.065 --> 00:02:16.605
the video game crash was in full swing.

00:02:16.605 --> 00:02:18.475
Disappointing sales and mismanagement

00:02:18.475 --> 00:02:20.626
forced Atari to restructure.

00:02:20.626 --> 00:02:22.110
- [Howard] And in the
next couple of months,

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:25.585
Atari went from 10,000
employees to 2,000 employees.

00:02:25.585 --> 00:02:27.137
- [Narrator] During the turmoil,

00:02:27.137 --> 00:02:28.832
Howard left the gaming industry for good.

00:02:28.832 --> 00:02:32.564
He didn't think much about E.T.
until nearly a decade later.

00:02:32.564 --> 00:02:33.812
- [Howard] Well, by the early 90s,

00:02:33.812 --> 00:02:36.954
people started coming out
with what a bad game E.T. was.

00:02:36.954 --> 00:02:38.766
I was just kinda surprised
anybody was actually

00:02:38.766 --> 00:02:40.772
even talking about it.

00:02:40.772 --> 00:02:43.196
- Good afternoon, some
of those E.T. Atari games

00:02:43.196 --> 00:02:47.452
unearthed at a New Mexico
landfill are now up for sale.

00:02:47.452 --> 00:02:49.762
Beaten up cartridges on
eBay are going for hundreds

00:02:49.762 --> 00:02:52.073
of dollars a piece.

00:02:52.073 --> 00:02:53.653
- So we had a poll online--

00:02:53.653 --> 00:02:54.657
- Yeah.

00:02:54.657 --> 00:02:57.020
- Where fans could choose
what game they wanted

00:02:57.020 --> 00:02:58.191
to see you play.

00:02:58.191 --> 00:02:59.024
- Okay.

00:02:59.024 --> 00:03:00.300
- They chose this.

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:02.921
E.T. the Extra-Terrestrial.

00:03:02.921 --> 00:03:05.396
- Even 30 years later, this obscure thing

00:03:05.396 --> 00:03:07.892
that I did so long ago in just five weeks,

00:03:07.892 --> 00:03:11.367
it's still generating
excitement, enthusiasm.

00:03:11.367 --> 00:03:13.255
I get emotional just talking about it now.

00:03:13.255 --> 00:03:16.278
Because I just felt I was
at the center of something

00:03:16.278 --> 00:03:18.418
that is still meaningful.

00:03:18.418 --> 00:03:21.348
And that is a huge success.

00:03:21.348 --> 00:03:23.552
So when people ask me, you
know, "Was E.T. a failure?"

00:03:23.552 --> 00:03:25.928
I don't mind when people call it that,

00:03:25.928 --> 00:03:29.255
but it'll never, it'll
never be a failure to me.

00:03:29.255 --> 00:03:32.005
(cheerful music)

